    description: "Structural scope-expansion from supply-verdict:F4 (Python in-tree module resolution, dev ba11d947a5, INV-nuzas import facet). Python's analyzer previously emitted 'imports' edges whose dst dangled to a phantom external_symbol boundary node for in-tree workspace-sibling modules (ast.Import did no lookup; 'from pkg import submod' failed on submodules). F4 fixed this for Python via a module_to_file_id map in py.py's analyze().\n\nVERIFY then FIX the analogue in the other in-tree-package languages: Go (module-path imports e.g. 'myorg/internal/foo'), Java/Kotlin (package imports). Question: do their analyzers resolve in-tree module imports to real first-party nodes, or do they dangle to external_symbol like Python did pre-F4? JS/TS already has linkers/js_module.py (no gap). Each language analyzer is separate (py.py/java.py/go.py each have their own _extract_import_edges-equivalent). If a gap exists, mirror F4's pattern (build an in-tree module->file-anchor map, resolve in the import-edge emitter). Behavioral check: run hypergumbo on a 2-package monorepo fixture per language and confirm sibling imports resolve to first_party file nodes, not external_dep boundary nodes. Filed as verify+fix (not yet confirmed broken)."  # b6b0
